# Cloudflare Workers Builds — dispatchos-coming-soon
# Connected: 2026-05-14
#
# This marker documents the Cloudflare "Workers Builds" GitHub integration that
# builds/deploys this Worker. It is configured in the Cloudflare dashboard
# (Worker -> Settings -> Builds), NOT in .github/workflows/* — nothing in this
# repo controls its trigger.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN ISSUE (2026-07-03): Workers Builds reds-out otherwise-green PRs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workers Builds is set to build on EVERY push, including PR branches. On PR
# commits its deploy step fails and posts a failing commit status named
#   "Workers Builds: dispatchos-coming-soon"   (app: cloudflare-workers-and-pages)
# which shows as a red check on the PR. It does NOT block merge (this private
# repo is on the free plan, so GitHub branch protection / rulesets are
# unavailable), but it makes green PRs look failed.
#
# It is also REDUNDANT: the real deploy already runs on push to develop/main via
# .github/workflows/deploy-worker.yml (which is correctly push-only, never on
# PRs). So the Worker has two deployers; only the CF one runs on PRs and fails.
#
# OWNER FIX (dashboard-only — cannot be done from this repo or via `gh`):
#   Preferred — remove the redundant + PR-noisy deployer entirely:
#     Cloudflare dashboard -> Workers & Pages -> dispatchos-coming-soon ->
#     Settings -> Builds -> DISCONNECT the GitHub Builds integration.
#     (The GitHub Actions workflow deploy-worker.yml keeps deploying on push.)
#   Alternative — keep CF Builds but stop it running on PRs:
#     Same Builds settings -> turn OFF "Non-production branch builds"
#     (a.k.a. build only the production branch), so PR/preview branches no longer
#     trigger a build+status.
#
# NOTE: this Worker is RETIRED from the apex (see wrangler.toml header) — the
# real dispatchos.co is served by Fly.io. The Worker is kept route-less/inert;
# its CF deploy failing on PRs has no production impact either way.
